"""COLLRAD H0 Maxwellian-averaged electron-impact ionization rate.

One leaf recovered from the saved KN1DPy ``collrad_sigmav_ion_h0`` slice
(source anchor ``KN1DPy/collrad_sigmav_ion_h0.py:16-217``, reachable from
``KN1DPy/kinetic_h.py:841-855`` when ``Use_Collrad_Ionization=1``), integrated
as :func:`evaluate_collrad_h0_ionization_rate`:

paired electron density ``n_e`` in ``m^-3`` and electron temperature ``T_e``
in eV map to the COLLRAD (DEGAS-2) H0 Maxwellian-averaged electron-impact
ionization rate in ``m^3/s`` by bilinear interpolation of ``log(rate)`` on the
frozen 15x60 ``log(n)``/``log(T)`` table.  Density converts internally to
``cm^-3``; the table coordinates are ``2 (log10(n) - 16)`` and
``10 (log10(T) + 1.2)``, each clamped to the table range, so endpoint values
are repeated outside it.

**The paired-sample order contract is the source's.**  Along the flat
C-order sequence of samples, the derived density and temperature table
coordinates must both be nondecreasing, matching the source's monotone
profile sweeps; an out-of-order pair is rejected.  The interpolation
arithmetic is preserved bit for bit.

**One repair relative to the saved implementation, documented.**  The saved
document declares the result shaped like the broadcast inputs, but its
implementation flattened everything to one dimension and compared flattened
sizes only, so two different-shaped arrays of the same size were silently
accepted and a two-dimensional profile came back flat.  This module requires
equal input shapes and returns the result with the inputs' shape.  Values on
one-dimensional inputs -- and the flattened values of higher-rank inputs --
are unchanged bit for bit.

Evidence ceiling: this module establishes the frozen table interpolation
against the saved package's grid anchors, the log-log bilinear midpoint as
the geometric mean of the four surrounding table corners, the endpoint
clamping, the input and failure contracts, the shape repair, and -- in the
environment-gated replay test -- exact agreement with the saved KN1DPy
implementation on one-dimensional sweeps.  The saved bundle's strict D1
qualification has been withdrawn; that replay is historical development
evidence, not qualified-L1 provenance.  The saved extraction ledger records a
source-runtime dataset validation (nine partitions, maximum relative error
1.45e-14) for the table itself; that is bounded historical evidence, not
independent physical-reference validation, and the saved package records the
external-reference residual as ``validation_oracle_gap``.  This module
evaluates no plasma state, no neutral transport and no source term; those are
caller-owned.
"""
